KDESIGEL v1.1 Readme File 
-----------------------

Contact: sigel@ls11.cs.uni-dortmund.de
This software is placed under the GNU General Public License.

Welcome to the world of Kdesigel. You want to use it? It's not as difficult
as baking pancake. Supported operating systems are Solaris (sparc)
and Linux (x86). All the following steps should be the same for both.

1. Building Kdesigel

If you don't have got a binary distribution and decided to build Kdesigel
by yourself, you need the gcc 2.95.2 or higher (get yours at www.gnu.org)
together with the appropriate libstdc++. The only thing you need which
maybe is not installed on every system is OpenGL. Its header files have
to be in the /usr/include/GL directory, the .so-files in /usr/openwin/lib
or /usr/X11R6/lib (in the Solaris respectively Linux case).

Assumed that you have untared the source-distribution, the best way to build Kdesigel is to use
kdevelop. First select build->autoconf/automake. After that klick on build->configure. Now you can
build Kdesigel with build->build.
If you want to build it without kdevelop, you should be familar with the autotools. After running
"autoconf" and "automake" (at least version 1.5) in the kdesigel-directory, you should
set "CXXFLAGS" to "-DQT_THREAD_SUPPORT -DQT_ALTERNATE_QTSMANIP -fpermissive"
and run "./configure". And it should build Kdesigel like kdevelop (tested on Suse and Debian).

Having build Kdesigel, you can delete everything but:
- the executables "sigel", "sigel_slave" and "manage_dyn_slave" (for dynamic clients, see below)
- the scripts "sigelLauncher", "povrayLauncher", "sigelDynClient" (for dynamic clients, see below)
- the file "Terrain.ter"
- the subdirectory pixmaps
- the subdirectory supportingLibs/pvm3

Then you should have quite the things as included in the binary distributions.
Or you could use the shell-script "./makeBinary" which copies all relevant files to
"/tmp/kdesigel".

2. Getting Kdesigel to work

First of all: Starting just the Kdesigel-executable without argument will start the
GUI-Version of Kdesigel.
- Starting it with the "-evolve <filename>" argument will start without a GUI
  and just evolves the given experiment until all individuals have got a valid fitnessvalue
  and the termination condition is reached.
- Starting it with "-devolve <filename>"  Kdesigel evolves with dynamic clients.
  Thus you can run Kdesigel for example in a batch system. For more information see below (2.6)

But whether you compiled Kdesigel by yourself oder downloaded a binary distribution, you have
first to do the following things to get Kdesigel to work:

2.1 Environment variables

Some environment variables have to be set in the shell-startup script (.cshrc for example in
the case you are using the C-Shell) of all hosts that want to take part in the parallel
evolution computations. The reason is that this variables have to be set when
the parallel evolution automatically open a remote shell on these hosts.

Variable                     Value

SIGEL_ROOT          Should contain the path to your sigel-installation directory.
PVM_ROOT             $SIGEL_ROOT/supportingLibs/pvm3
PVM_ARCHDIR      LINUX (for linux ;-)

If you only want to start Kdesigel locally, you could also use the script "sigelLauncher", which
sets all the environment variables (see below 2.3).

2.2 The .rhosts file or using ssh for a remote-shell

One method to start evolution computations on other hosts is the use of the rsh (remote-shell) mechanism.
That means that:
- This service has to be offered by the host.
- An appropriate .rhosts file has to grant Kdesigel acces without a password.

Example: If you want to start the Kdesigel-master process on a host named "dagobah" and your
loginname on this host is "yoda", while you want to use a host called "hoth" for Kdesigel-slave
computations. Then the .rhosts file on "hoth" has to contain the following line:

dagobah yoda

That means that the user "yoda" may log into from "dagobah" into "hoth" without giving
a password using the rsh mechanism. You better be careful with the .rhosts file. You find
more info on that typing "man rhosts".

Because of the security issues of r-commands, PVM and thus Kdesigel supports the use of
ssh. Therefore you have to set the environment variable

PVM_RSH=path to ssh

in addition to the environment variables in section 2.1. After this kdesigel starts the slave on
other clients with ssh. You should set up ssh thus you can login to the clients without specifing
a password.
It is important to mention it again, if you use Kdesigel with automatic evolution you must set
the environment variables in your shell configuration file, else it will not run.

2.3 The sigelLauncher script

This script sigelLauncher does the setting of the variables decribed above. It does not
replace the step 2.1, but is written for the case you want to start sigel through a cronjob.
The cronjob starts in a "naked" shell. The script contains one line setting the SIGEL_ROOT
variable. This is the only line you need to modify. Then you can use it for example in the
folowing crontab-entry:

00      21      20      8       *       /home/sawitzki/sigelLauncher -evolve insectNiceWalkingFitness.exp

2.4 Using gnuplot

It is possible to plot the fitnesscurves with gnuplot directly from Kdesigel. This of course
works only if gnuplot is found in a directory given in your PATH-variable.

2.5 PovrayLauncher

The Kdesigel distribution contains a script called "povrayLauncher" which calls Povray for
all .pov-files found in the current directory. Arguments given to the script are reached
to Povray, which has of course to be found in a directory given in your PATH-variable.

2.6 Dynamic clients and sigelDynClient

This script is used in combination with the dynamic client facility of Kdesigel. It uses the
program "manage_dyn_slave" which registers itself as a client over a socket to
a Kdesigel-master, which was started with the argument "-devolve ...". The usage is

sigelDynClient <host name of the master>

The script first copies the Kdesigel-binary Distribution to "/tmp/_SIGEL_EVOLUTION_TEMP". After
that it registers itself  to the master (manage_dyn_slave) and waits for jobs to calculate the fitness.
After every generation the master disconnects all dynamic clients, thus the master and pvm could reschedule
the fitness calculation. At the end the script removes the directory "/tmp/_SIGEL_EVOLUTION_TEMP".

To get this working you have to do the following:

1) First of all you should have compiled Kdesigel (described in section 1).

2) Now it is important to set the environment variables as described in section 2.1. But you must
    set SIGEL_ROOT to "/tmp/_SIGEL_EVOLUTION_TEMP", because there must be the binary Distribution
     located.

3) Set the .rhosts or the PVM_RSH variable appropiate (section 2.2).

4) Copy the binary Distribution to your local directory (/home/"your name") on the clients (section 2). Rename the
    directory to "_SIGEL_EVOLUTION_TEMP". [You get the binary distribution either by downloading it from sourceforge
    or using the script "makeBinary", which creates a tar.gz-file in /tmp (you have to unpack it into your home directory).]

5) Copy "manage_dyn_slave" and "sigelDynClient" to your local directory.

6) Set up your experiment. The static host of this experiments must have the same
    directory path as the script (thus "/tmp/_SIGEL_EVOLUTION_TEMP") and the host is the computer you
    want to start the master.

7) Start Kdesigel on your local host, where the static host is defined (see above 6) with
     "sigel -devolve <EXPERIMENT>".

8) As soon as "awaiting clients" is shown, you could start the dynamic clients. Either
    manually with the "sigelDynClient"-script on every client or more comfortable
    with a batch system:
    "bsub -o /dev/null -q normal -m "<YOURHOSTS>" "sigelDynClient <MASTERHOST>" "

